{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Buggy Deep Scan",
  "description": "User-triggered project-wide bug hunt across Python files. This one DOES execute your code, so it asks which paths to cover before it starts.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "userTriggered"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "The user triggered a deep scan. Unlike the automatic hooks, this one executes project code, so establish scope before running anything.\n\nPhase 1 - inventory, nothing executed:\n1. Find the project's .py files, excluding virtualenvs, site-packages, build output and caches.\n2. Call buggy_functions on each to get its functions and which are investigable. This only parses.\n3. Call buggy_recall once, with no 'function', to list every defect already on record.\n\nPhase 2 - confirm scope with the user:\nbuggy_investigate and buggy_scan import the target module, which runs its top-level code, and investigating an instance method constructs the receiver with generated arguments. In a project that opens database connections, calls an API, or writes files at import time, that really happens. Before executing anything, show the user the inventory and ask which paths to cover. Recommend starting with pure-logic modules - parsing, validation, numeric and date arithmetic, formatting - and holding back anything that touches the network, the filesystem or a database.\n\nPhase 3 - investigate the approved paths only:\n4. For each approved file, call buggy_scan for a one-line-per-function triage, or buggy_investigate per function when the specification matters. Conditions are PYTHON expressions, for example postconditions ['result >= 0', 'math.isfinite(result)'] and preconditions ['denominator != 0'].\n5. Infer each specification from the code: a return annotation implies a postcondition about the result, a docstring that documents a raise implies an expected exception, division implies a non-zero denominator, indexing implies bounds, money implies a non-negative result.\n\nPhase 4 - report:\n6. Write a summary to .buggy/deep-scan-report.md containing the date, which paths were covered and which were skipped and why, functions investigated, defects proven with their runnable 'reproduce' call, and which files carry the most findings.\n7. Count statuses honestly. 'proven' is a real reproducible defect. 'unconfirmed' means nothing failed within the budget and is NOT a clean bill of health. 'no_valid_input' means the function never ran at all.\n\nPresent the summary when complete."
  }
}
